fully unprepared for this  pandemic and elementary things like  producing enough  um protective personal equipment i mean  where i live people actually went round  to the local hospital and started making  it themselves  we really should have uh uh have been  ready to  deal with problems elementary problems  like that and we weren't and cummings  was quite right about that yeah what i  mean what why do you think you  you believed a lot of what he said i  mean he is in some respects a man  out for revenge isn't it the man who's  been scorned  he is and i think that's one reason why  it probably won't damage the government  that much because  if there's one person who people in the  wider  um country have heard of it's dominic  cummings and many people are so  extremely angry about um his  his expedition to daruman to barnard  castle so  i think actually and and also it's a bit  like  this oddly enough i think the psychology  is a bit like the second world war  in the second world war we had all sorts  of appalling disasters  for several years um not only under  neville chamberlain but under winston  churchill and then  we won the war and everyone felt so glad  that we won the war of course so sad at  the terrible casualty which would occur  but the the the joy of winning  um somehow eclipsed the anger  at the extreme waste of life and  and so on which had occurred uh as we  worked our way as we learned how to win  that victory and i think  something of the same feeling people  feel so good about the vaccinations  that they are to some extent prepared to  forgive  um the the terrible mistakes which were  made earlier also  cummings is always repair making this  comparison between  what happened and some sort of rather  illusory idea of a perfect response  which admittedly he says you know they  did it very very well in taiwan  and we weren't prepared to learn from  taiwan and south korea  um and singapore um but  the wider public know that life is never  perfect the g